<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Podlachia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Podlasie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Podlasie is a historical region in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, centred on Białystok and bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>. It formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569 transferred it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-of-the-kingdom-of-poland">Crown of Poland</a>. Its mixed Polish, Belarusian, Lithuanian, Jewish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> population made it a meeting point of Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> traditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/podlachia.jpg" alt="Podlachia" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marek Mróz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2019-07_Tykocin_(9).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The region lies along the middle stretch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-bug">Bug River</a>, between Mazovia in the west, Polesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> in the east, the Narew River in the north and the Chełm Land in the south. Its largest city is Białystok and its historical capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drohiczyn">Drohiczyn</a>. The name is probably from an East Slavic word for a Pole, meaning near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, from the period when the territory lay on the Mazovian border of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Masovians settled the west before the 11th century, and Yotvingians held the north in the 12th until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-v-the-chaste">Bolesław V the Chaste</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-black">Leszek II the Black</a> drove them out in the 13th and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> repopulated the land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> contested the region from then on, and in 1253 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> recognised Polish sovereignty. It returned to the Grand Duchy in 1446. In 1513 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a> formed the Podlaskie Voivodeship, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drohiczyn">Drohiczyn</a>, divided into the lands of Drohiczyn, Mielnik and Bielsk.</p><p>Knyszyn was the favourite residence of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a>, who died there in 1572, and Tykocin was the chief royal residence in the 17th and early 18th centuries. In the 18th and 19th centuries Białystok became the main centre, thanks to the Branicki family and its textile industry.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition</a> of 1795 divided Podlasie between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, with the Bug as the border. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaties of Tilsit</a> in 1807 part of it joined the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and part, including Białystok, came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and in 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> placed all of it under Russian control. Two of the largest battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising">January Uprising</a>, at Węgrów and Siemiatycze, were fought there in 1863. It became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after the Treaty of Riga of 1921, was split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1939, and returned to Polish control in 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The modern Podlaskie Voivodeship, established in 1999, covers the northern part of the historical region, including Białystok and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drohiczyn">Drohiczyn</a>; Podlasie makes up 36% of it. The region is mostly inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, with many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a> in the east, and has long been considered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s most culturally diverse region. It is also a cultural centre of Poland's small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> minority, and it has its own folk costumes, traditional architecture and cuisine, of which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> babka is the best known dish.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Podlasie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Union of Lublin in 1569 King Sigismund II Augustus transferred <b>Podlasie</b>, Volhynia, Kiev and Bracław to the Crown of Poland, and the grand duchy entered the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth with a common monarch, diet and foreign policy but its own army, treasury, offices and law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>This gave the Polish nobility the leverage to insist on terms favourable to the Crown, including the transfer of <b>Podlachia</b>, Volhynia and the large Ukrainian palatinates of Kiev and Bracław from Lithuanian to Polish administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
